            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Rockwell tools?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Modern Rockwell power tools are designed and marketed by Positec Tool Corporation, the same Chinese parent company that owns WORX. Rockwell and WORX are corporate siblings sharing engineering and manufacturing. Note that this brand has no connection to the historic Rockwell/Delta industrial machinery, to Rockwell Automation, or to Rockwell Collins — Positec simply uses the Rockwell name for its consumer power tool line.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Rockwell tools good quality?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              For DIY and homeowner use, yes. Rockwell's signature products like the Sonicrafter, Versacut, and JawHorse are well-designed and competent. However, the tools are not professional-grade — the budget and cordless lines are plastic-heavy and built for intermittent use rather than daily jobsite abuse. Quality is appropriate to the price and purpose, not deficient, but it is clearly tuned for occasional users.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Rockwell the same as WORX?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Effectively, yes — both are owned and produced by Positec, share engineering and battery technology, and target the same value-focused homeowner segment. WORX is the more prominent consumer brand, especially in outdoor power equipment, while Rockwell leans on its signature innovation products. Neither is meaningfully more professional than the other; the choice usually comes down to price, battery platform, and specific features.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the Rockwell Sonicrafter?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              The Sonicrafter is Rockwell's oscillating multi-tool, and it is the brand's most famous and best-regarded product. Positec's predecessor work helped pioneer the entire oscillating multi-tool category. The Sonicrafter handles plunge cuts, flush cuts, grout and adhesive scraping, detail sanding, and trim work. It remains one of the better values in the multi-tool category and is the Rockwell product we recommend most readily.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Rockwell good for professional use?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Generally no. Rockwell is built for DIYers, homeowners, and occasional users, not for heavy daily professional duty. Tradespeople and contractors are better served by professional ecosystems like Milwaukee, DeWalt, or Makita, which offer brushless motors, metal-reinforced construction, larger battery platforms, and service networks. Rockwell shines when you want a specific innovative tool affordably, not as an all-day jobsite workhorse.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Rockwell vs. Ryobi — which is better?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              They occupy nearly the same DIY-value position. Ryobi's big advantage is its ONE+ battery platform, an enormous ecosystem of well over a hundred tools that share one battery, with strong retail availability. For building out a broad cordless collection over time, Ryobi is hard to beat. Rockwell's edge is its standout individual products — choose Ryobi for platform breadth, choose Rockwell for tools like the Sonicrafter, Versacut, and JawHorse.
            </p>
          </div>
